The '''2026 United States Senate election in Alaska''' was held on November 3, 2026, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent the state of [[Alaska]]. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Dan Sullivan (U.S. senator)|Dan S. Sullivan]] ran for a third term, but lost to [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] former U.S. representative [[Mary Peltola]]. The election was scheduled to be completed under [[instant-runoff voting]] on November 18 if no candidate had received more than 50 percent of the vote in the first round, but no tabulation was required due to Peltola receiving an outright majority of the votes, and the election was called for Peltola officially on November 17.

A [[nonpartisan primary]] election was held on August 18 to determine the four candidates in the general election. Peltola and Sullivan advanced with 49.5% and 41.4% of the vote respectively, followed by Republican former teacher Dan J. Sullivan and Republican [[perennial candidate]] Gerald Heikes.

Dan J. Sullivan's candidacy drew allegations of being a [[Spoiler effect|spoiler]] due to having the same first and last name as the incumbent, but he was allowed to remain on the ballot by the state Supreme Court and was listed as unaffiliated (although he had originally filed for candidacy as a Republican). The Trump administration subsequently filed a lawsuit to block the election results if Dan S. Sullivan lost by a small margin. The lawsuit was rejected but is under appeal in the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/11/01/federal-judge-rejects-trump-administration-attempt-to-pause-alaskas-us-senate-election/ |title=Federal judge rejects Trump administration attempt to pause Alaska's US Senate election |first=James |last=Brooks |date=November 1, 2026 |work=Alaska Beacon}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Brooks |first=James |date=October 23, 2026 |title=Trump administration sues to block Alaska's US Senate election result if Peltola wins |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/10/23/trump-administration-sues-to-block-alaskas-us-senate-election-result-if-peltola-wins/ |access-date=November 15, 2026 |website=Alaska Beacon |language=en-US}}</ref> Peltola will be the first Democrat to win a U.S. Senate election in Alaska since [[Mark Begich]] in [[2008 United States Senate election in Alaska|2008]], the second Native American woman elected to the U.S. Senate alongside [[Peggy Flanagan]] and the first [[Alaska Natives|Alaska Native]] senator.

This was one of eight seats Democrats flipped in a state carried by [[Donald Trump]] in the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]].

