==Primary election==
Under Alaska's voting system, all candidates, regardless of party, run on the same primary ballot. The top four candidates in the primary advance to the general election.<ref>{{cite web |title=Top-four primary |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Top-four_primary |website=Ballotpedia |access-date=May 19, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> There is no ranked choice in the primary election: each voter selects a single candidate.
=== Background ===
Incumbent Republican [[Dan Sullivan (U.S. senator)|Dan S. Sullivan]] announced his run for re-election in early 2025. He was first elected in [[2014 United States Senate election in Alaska|2014]], defeating [[Mark Begich]], the most recent Democrat to represent Alaska in the [[U.S. Senate]]. He was last re-elected in [[2020 United States Senate election in Alaska|2020]], prior to the implementation of Alaska's ranked-choice voting system.<ref name=":0" /> Facing few obvious swing state targets to regain the Senate majority in [[2026 United States Senate elections|2026]], Democrats began looking towards more Republican-leaning states, such as [[2026 United States Senate special election in Ohio|Ohio]] and [[2026 United States Senate election in Texas|Texas]], as potential targets.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Neukam |first=Stephen |date=August 6, 2025 |title=Senate Democrats' top remaining targets for the 2026 midterms |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/08/06/senate-democrats-2026-elections-candidates |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref> Among these states was Alaska, where Senate Democratic leader [[Chuck Schumer]] heavily courted former U.S. Representative [[Mary Peltola]], the last Democrat to win any statewide election in Alaska, to run for Senate.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Nichols |first1=Hans |last2=Neukam |first2=Stephen |date=August 13, 2025 |title=Scoop: Schumer courts Peltola for Alaska Senate race |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/08/13/alaska-senate-peltola-schumer-sullivan |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref> Peltola narrowly lost her congressional seat in 2024, and had considered running for either Senate or [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election|governor]], but she ultimately decided to enter the Senate race in early 2026, setting up the race between her and Sullivan.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Herz |first=Nathaniel |date=July 15, 2025 |title=Dems are 'salivating' over a Peltola bid for Senate, but Alaska governor race could be 'wide open' |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/07/15/dems-are-salivating-over-a-peltola-bid-for-senate-but-alaska-governor-race-could-be-wide-open/ |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=Alaska Beacon |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Thiessen |first=Mark |date=January 12, 2026 |title=Democrat Mary Peltola, barrier-breaking ex-Alaska congresswoman, is challenging Republican Dan Sullivan in Senate race |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/democrat-mary-peltola-barrier-breaking-ex-alaska-congresswoman-is-challenging-republican-dan-sullivan-in-senate-race |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=PBS News |language=en-us}}</ref>

In July 2026, the Alaska Division of Elections moved 3,048 voters to "inactive" on its voter rolls based in part on flawed and outdated federal data indicating they might not be citizens. The Alaska Division of Motor Vehicles often includes citizens who do not reach back out to update their citizenship status to the DMV after [[naturalization]]. Historically, the Alaska DMV provided 300 names every three months for verification. Voters can still vote with a 'Question ballot' attesting that they are citizens but have to fill out additional paperwork for their vote to count.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/2026-07-23/state-removes-3-500-alaskans-from-voter-rolls-based-in-part-on-old-data|title=State removes 3,000 Alaskans from voter rolls, based in part on 'old data'|first=Liz|last=Ruskin|website=[[Alaska Public Media]]|date=July 23, 2026|access-date=October 6, 2026}}</ref> 700 Alaskans were able to restore their status on the active list as of August 12, 2026.<ref name="Ruskin2026-1">{{Cite web |last=Ruskin |first=Liz |date=August 12, 2026 |title=Alaska's delisting of voters may violate federal law, Legislature's legal advisor says |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/2026-08-12/alaskas-delisting-of-voters-may-violate-federal-law-legislatures-legal-advisor-says |access-date=November 9, 2026 |website=Alaska Public Media |language=en}}</ref> The department said it was able to deregister 100 voters who were not citizens and who wished to be taken off the list. They may have been some of the noncitizens added by mistake by DMV employees. The state was accused of violating federal law by some state legislators and the state chapter of the ACLU by conducting a "systematic purge" of active voter rolls within 90 days of an election.<ref name="Ruskin2026-1" />

====Candidacy of Dan J. Sullivan and controversy of names====
'''Daniel James Sullivan Jr.''' (born June 1957)<ref name="Bohrer2026a">{{Cite web |last=Bohrer |first=Becky |date=June 9, 2026 |title=The other Dan Sullivan in Alaska's US Senate race says candidacy was 'my choice,' not a sham |url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-senate-dan-sullivan-peltola-68ca38749253c6bf52d13051fda01251 |access-date=June 27, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en|quote=}}</ref> is a retired teacher and [[United States Forest Service]] employee from [[Petersburg, Alaska]], who filed to run as a Republican three days before the filing deadline.<ref name="SullivanPressRelease">{{Cite press release |title=Dan Sullivan Challenges Dan Sullivan for U.S. Senate Seat: Urges Alaskans to defeat incumbent, elect a Sullivan who Stands up for Alaska |date=May 29, 2026 |publisher=Sullivan for Senate |url=https://www.sullivanforsenate.com/press |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260529204149/https://www.sullivanforsenate.com/press |archive-date=May 29, 2026}}</ref> Daniel James Sullivan and Daniel Scott Sullivan are unrelated to each other and are both unrelated to former [[List of mayors of Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage mayor]] [[Dan Sullivan (Anchorage mayor)|Daniel Albert Sullivan]] (in office 2009–2015).<ref>{{Cite web |last=DeMarban |first=Alex |title=Namesake challenger to Sen. Dan Sullivan remains defiant despite state investigation |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/06/10/namesake-challenger-to-sen-dan-sullivan-remains-defiant-despite-state-investigation/ |access-date=June 27, 2026 |website=Anchorage Daily News |date=June 10, 2026 |quote=}}</ref>

In an interview with the ''[[Petersburg Pilot]]'', Dan J. Sullivan stated that he had been considering running against the incumbent U.S. senator for 12 years, and remarked that he finally decided to run after the Senator's lack of opposition to the [[Anti-Weaponization Fund]].<ref name="Morrow2026a"/> Senator Sullivan said this was "an effort to confuse Alaskans" and "dirty politics" by Democrats, calling Dan J. Sullivan a "sham candidate". This was denied by Dan J. Sullivan, who called himself a "pragmatic Republican centrist". It was also denied by Mary Peltola's campaign, the Alaska Democratic Party, and the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee. Senator Sullivan threatened legal action if the [[Lieutenant Governor of Alaska]] did not remove Dan J. Sullivan from the ballot.<ref name="cnn dan sullivan">{{Cite web |last1=Raju |first1=Ranu |last2=Fayez |first2=Aleena |date=June 7, 2026 |title=GOP Sen. Dan Sullivan is raising alarms about an eleventh-hour challenger: Dan J. Sullivan |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/07/politics/dan-sullivan-alaska-senate-ballot |access-date=June 7, 2026 |website=CNN |language=en-us}}</ref>

Dan J. Sullivan grew up in the [[Chicago metropolitan area]], and has lived in [[Southeast Alaska]] since 1980. He worked for the [[United States Forest Service]] in his early career before beginning a career as an elementary school teacher.<ref name="Bohrer2026a" /> He taught in elementary schools for over 20 years before retiring.<ref name="Heckart2026a">{{Cite web |last=Heckart |first=Taylor |date=June 4, 2026 |title=It's no joke: A second Dan Sullivan says he really does want to be Alaska's next senator |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/elections/2026-06-04/its-no-joke-a-second-dan-sullivan-says-he-really-does-want-to-be-alaskas-next-senator |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=Alaska Public Media}}</ref> He was appointed to the city of Petersburg's Public Safety Advisory Board in December 2021,<ref name="Heckart2026a"/> and he served in that position until October 2022, and he is listed on the Petersburg government website as "Dan Sullivan".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Dan Sullivan |url=https://www.petersburgak.gov/directory-listing/dan-sullivan |access-date=June 27, 2026 |website=Petersburg, Alaska |language=en}}</ref> He stated that he was a member of the [[Alaskan Independence Party]] from when he moved to Alaska in 1980 until the party's dissolution in late 2025.<ref name="Heckart2026a" /><ref name="Morrow2026a">{{Cite web |last=Morrow |first=Caleb |date=June 4, 2026 |title=Petersburg's Dan Sullivan challenges Senator Dan Sullivan |url=HTTPS://www.petersburgpilot.com/story/2026/06/04/news/petersburgs-dan-sullivan-challenges-senator-dan-sullivan/15854.html |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=Petersburg Pilot}}</ref> 

Dan J. Sullivan's initial campaign announcement stated that he was "focused on unseating the incumbent, putting Alaska first, and restoring accountability, common sense, and public service to Washington, D.C."<ref name="SullivanPressRelease"/> He later described his political philosophy as "a pragmatic Republican centrist" similar to [[Lisa Murkowski]], with influence from [[Rand Paul]].<ref name="Bohrer2026a" /> Incumbent U.S. Senator Daniel Scott Sullivan argued in an early June 2026 interview with [[CNN]] that "[Daniel James Sullivan's] whole purpose of running is to confuse Alaskans, to make him, make them think – Alaskan voters – that somehow he's me, so they could rig the vote in favor of [[Mary Peltola]]."<ref name="cnn dan sullivan"/> An analysis of [[Federal Election Commission]] records by [[KTUU-TV]] showed that he had donated to Peltola's previous campaigns, including $100 in 2022 and $30 in 2024. KTUU reported, "That financial history is the extent of the documented link between the Petersburg Sullivan and the Peltola campaign."<ref name="CourtneySimon2026">{{Cite web |last1=Courtney |first1=Wil |last2=Simon |first2=Matthew |date=June 13, 2026 |title=Two Dan Sullivans: Petersburg candidate poised for removal from ballot by state |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2026/06/13/two-dan-sullivans-petersburg-candidate-poised-removal-ballot-by-state/ |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=KTUU-TV}}</ref> His initial press release and website design were supported by political strategist Amber Lee, who has previously worked with Democratic campaigns in Alaska.<ref name="Courtney2026a">{{Cite web |last=Courtney |first=Wil |date=June 4, 2026 |title=Sullivan vs. Sullivan: Senator accuses Peltola of 'rigging' election — but evidence of her involvement is unclear |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2026/06/04/sullivan-vs-sullivan-senator-accuses-peltola-rigging-election-evidence-her-involvement-is-unclear/ |access-date=June 29, 2026 |work=KTUU-TV}}</ref> Lee has publicly spoken in favor of Peltola's candidacy, including in a January 2026 interview with [[The Hill (newspaper)|''The Hill'']].<ref name="Courtney2026a" /><ref name="ElissaSlotkin">{{Cite web |last=Mueller |first=Julia |date=January 15, 2026 |title=Peltola's entrance in Alaska Senate race gives Democrats hope |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5689076-alaska-senate-race-democrats/ |access-date=June 29, 2026 |work=The Hill}}</ref>

''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'' reported that weeks before the election, polls showed that approximately one third of voters likely to vote in the primary election could not distinguish the two candidates.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=August 13, 2014 |title=Alaska Election Offers Two Chances to Vote for Dan Sullivan |url=http://online.wsj.com/articles/alaska-election-offers-two-chances-to-vote-for-dan-sullivan-1407897002 |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=Wall Street Journal |issn=0099-9660}}</ref> Dan J. Sullivan's candidacy was the subject of legal debate, which {{As of|2026|6|27|df=US|lc=y}} advanced to the [[Alaska Supreme Court]].<ref name="Ruskin2026a">{{Cite web |last=Ruskin |first=Liz |date=June 27, 2026 |title=Judge puts 2nd Dan Sullivan back on ballot in Alaska's U.S. Senate race |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/2026-06-26/judge-puts-2nd-dan-sullivan-back-on-ballot-in-alaskas-u-s-senate-race |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=Alaska Public Media}}</ref> In its initial response to Sullivan's candidacy filing, the Alaska Division of Elections stated that Sullivan's name would appear on the ballot as "Sullivan, Dan J. (Registered Republican)".<ref name="Hanson2026a">{{Cite web |last=Hanson |first=Lars |date=June 27, 2026 |title=Judge orders Petersburg's Dan J. Sullivan onto Alaska U.S. Senate primary ballot |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2026/06/27/judge-orders-petersburgs-dan-j-sullivan-onto-alaska-us-senate-primary-ballot/ |access-date=June 27, 2026 |work=[[KTUU-TV]]}}</ref>

The Alaska Director of Elections ordered Sullivan's name removed from the ballot, saying his candidacy "was not filed in order to declare an actual good-faith candidacy for the office of United States Senator, but was instead filed with a purpose to confuse or mislead and to thereby compromise the ballot's fairness or neutrality",<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ruskin |first=Liz |date=June 15, 2026 |title=Alaska Elections Division makes it official, says Dan J. Sullivan can't run against Sen. Dan S. Sullivan |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/2026-06-15/alaska-elections-division-makes-it-official-says-dan-j-sullivan-cant-run-against-sen-dan-s-sullivan |access-date=June 29, 2026 |work=Alaska Public Media}}</ref> a decision he appealed.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5936869-dan-sullivan-ballot-challenge|title=Alaska Senate candidate with same name as Sullivan sues to stay on primary ballot |last=Rego|first=Max|date=June 23, 2026|access-date=November 7, 2026|work=The Hill}}</ref> On June 26, Anchorage Superior Court judge Thomas A. Matthews reversed the Division of Elections' decision to exclude Sullivan from the primary ballot. Matthews' decision ruled that the "good-faith" standard for ballot access was not supported by Alaska law, the Division of Elections' regulations, or [[Article One of the United States Constitution#Section 3: Senate|Article One, Section 3 of the United States Constitution]].<ref name="Hanson2026a"/><ref name="Ruskin2026a" /><ref name="DeMarban2026a">{{Cite web |last=DeMarban |first=Alex |date=June 27, 2026 |title=Judge allows namesake challenger to Sen. Dan Sullivan to appear on Alaska ballot |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/06/26/superior-court-judge-rules-in-favor-of-namesake-challenger-to-sen-dan-sullivan/ |access-date=June 29, 2026 |work=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref> On July 2, the [[Alaska Supreme Court]] affirmed the decision, ordering Sullivan's name to be listed on the August 18 primary ballot.<ref name="adn supreme court">{{Cite web |last=DeMarban |first=Alex |title=Alaska Supreme Court allows namesake challenger to U.S. Sen. Dan Sullivan to appear on the ballot |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/06/29/alaska-supreme-court-rules-that-dan-j-sullivan-can-appear-on-the-ballot-against-sen-dan-sullivan/ |access-date=July 2, 2026 |website=Anchorage Daily News}}</ref> On June 29,<!-- The ADN article says the sample ballot was released "on Monday", which would have been the 29th --> the Division of Elections released a sample ballot{{clarify |date=November 2026 |reason=Is this what was used on the final ballot, or was the June sample ballot release just something that happened before the final ballot format was established? How were these candidates identified on the final ballot? }} that distinguished between the two Sullivans by listing Dan J. Sullivan as "Sullivan, Daniel J. Jr.", with no party affiliation listed (despite Sullivan filing as a Republican) and using "Daniel" rather than "Dan" for him and adding "Jr."<!-- there was no "Jr." in the initial announced listing -->, and identifying Dan S. Sullivan as "Dan S. Sullivan (Registered Republican) Incumbent", specifically identifying him as the incumbent, which they had not done for any other incumbent for at least 10 years.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/06/30/election-officials-sample-ballot-shows-how-they-seek-to-distinguish-two-dan-sullivans-in-us-senate-race/ |title=Election officials' sample ballot shows how they seek to distinguish two Dan Sullivans in U.S. Senate race |first=Alex |last=DeMarban |date=June 30, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-administration-files-lawsuit-to-block-possible-democratic-win-in-alaska-senate-race |title=WATCH: Trump says he ordered DOJ lawsuit over Alaska Senate race |first1=Nicholas |last1=Riccardi |date=October 23, 2026 |via=PBS News |agency=Associated Press |access-date=November 6, 2026}}<!-- How is this citation relevant? It does not mention the sample ballot and appears to have been published before the sample ballot was released. --></ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Balk |first1=Tim |last2=Browning |first2=Kellen |date=October 23, 2026 |title=Justice Dept. Suit Reignites a Legal Fight in Alaska |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/23/us/alaska-courts-ruled-months-ago-on-the-two-dan-sullivans-in-the-senate-race.html |access-date=October 24, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |url-access=subscription }}<!-- How is this citation relevant to this sentence about the sample ballot --></ref>

=== Campaign ===
According to most predictors, Peltola's January entry into the race immediately made the race competitive.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kondik |first=Kyle |date=January 13, 2026 |title=Alaska Senate Race Comes Onto the Competitive Board with Peltola's Entry |url=https://centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/alaska-senate-race-comes-onto-the-competitive-board-with-peltolas-entry/ |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=Sabato's Crystal Ball |language=en-US}}</ref> She still faced significant hurdles, such as Sullivan's incumbency, the lack of a prominent Republican challenger, the unpopularity of the national Democratic Party in Alaska, and a reduction in cross-party support. [[Lisa Murkowski]], Alaska's other U.S. Senator and a noted [[moderate]] Republican, had endorsed Peltola in her [[2022 United States House of Representatives election in Alaska|2022]] and [[2024 United States House of Representatives election in Alaska|2024]] bids for U.S. House, but she endorsed Dan S. Sullivan in this race instead.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 12, 2026 |title=Former Rep. Mary Peltola jumps into Alaska Senate race |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/01/12/peltola-alaska-senate-2026-midterms-00721596 |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> However, as Trump's unpopularity grew, especially as the [[2026 Iran war]] began and continued, Peltola's chances of flipping the Senate seat grew.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Fischler |first1=Jacob|last2=Shorman|first2=Jonathan |date=June 18, 2026 |title=Alaska among 9 states to decide if Dems flip US Senate |url=https://www.juneauindependent.com/post/alaska-among-9-states-to-decide-if-dems-flip-us-senate |access-date=November 13, 2026 |work=Juneau Independent|agency=Alaska Beacon |language=en}}</ref>

Over the course of the primary campaign, a [[super PAC]] called Fair Deal Alaska, registered to a [[virtual office]] address in [[Wasilla, Alaska|Wasilla]], spent around $770,000 promoting Green Party candidate [[Richard Grayson (writer)|Richard Grayson]] and Democrat [[Carol Hafner]] in what the ''[[Alaska Beacon]]'' and the ''[[Anchorage Daily News]]'' described as an apparent attempt to dilute support for Peltola in the general election.<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":5">{{Cite news |last=Samuels |first=Iris |date=August 13, 2026 |title=Millions pour into Alaska to boost fringe U.S. Senate candidates |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/13/millions-pour-into-alaska-to-boost-fringe-us-senate-candidates/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260814003724/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/13/millions-pour-into-alaska-to-boost-fringe-us-senate-candidates/ |archive-date=August 14, 2026 |access-date=August 13, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]]}}</ref> Another PAC called American Democracy Project spent over $240,000 to promote Democrat David Leslie.<ref name=":5" /> A similar group called Right for Alaska spent $1.2 million promoting Republican Gerald Heikes and [[Alaskan Party]] candidate Earl "Skip" Southworth, who were considered more likely to draw support from Dan S. Sullivan.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Herz |first=Nathaniel |date=August 3, 2026 |title=As Alaska's U.S. Senate campaign heats up, super PACs boost out-of-state candidates |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/08/03/as-alaskas-u-s-senate-campaign-heats-up-super-pacs-boost-out-of-state-candidates/ |work=[[Alaska Beacon]]}}</ref><ref name=":5" /> Both Grayson and Leslie expressed concern about the outside spending on their behalf to the ''Anchorage Daily News''. Grayson—a [[satirical candidate]] living in Arizona—said that he supported Peltola and pledged to withdraw if he advanced to the general election.<ref name=":5" />

