== General election ==
===Campaign===
Democratic candidate David Leslie placed fourth in the primary. Leslie, a [[democratic socialist]] who had criticized Peltola's stances on the [[Gaza genocide]], environmental protection, and actions by [[U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE), initially said he would not withdraw but later reversed course, citing "pressure from not just the Alaska Democratic leadership, but many different institutions of power".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Herz |first1=Nathaniel |title=This Democratic Socialist could cost Mary Peltola votes in November. But he says he'd actually boost her. |url=https://anchoragepress.com/this-democratic-socialist-could-cost-mary-peltola-votes-in-november-but-he-says-hed-actually-boost-her/ |access-date=September 27, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Press]] |date=August 21, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260903140950/https://anchoragepress.com/this-democratic-socialist-could-cost-mary-peltola-votes-in-november-but-he-says-hed-actually-boost-her/ |archive-date=September 3, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="DropsOut">{{cite news |last1=DeMarban |first1=Alex |title=Alaska's 4th-place U.S. Senate finisher drops out, elevating GOP candidate to ballot |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/01/alaskas-4th-place-us-senate-finisher-drops-out-elevating-gop-candidate-to-ballot/ |access-date=September 27, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=September 1, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260922113331/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/01/alaskas-4th-place-us-senate-finisher-drops-out-elevating-gop-candidate-to-ballot/ |archive-date=September 22, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Leslie's withdrawal allowed Republican [[perennial candidate]] Gerald Heikes to advance to the general election.<ref name="DropsOut" /> Democratic-aligned groups spent over $2 million on ads promoting Heikes during the campaign. Heikes said that the ads used [[AI-generated imagery]], but otherwise accurately presented his political views. Heikes had initially planned to run for US House in 2026, but said he decided to enter the Senate race after being promised support by a political operative from the [[Midwestern United States|Midwest]].<ref name="Trump">{{cite news |last1=DeMarban |first1=Alex |title=Trump weighs in on Alaska's tight U.S. Senate race as thousands of ballots are yet to be tallied |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/11/05/trump-weighs-in-on-alaskas-tight-us-senate-race-as-thousands-of-ballots-are-yet-to-be-tallied/ |access-date=November 9, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=November 5, 2026}}</ref>

On September 23, ''[[The New York Times]]'' published allegations from anonymous aides who had worked for Peltola's congressional office and Senate campaign. The report included text messages showing that Peltola had used an ableist slur and expressed hope that a board member of a local [[Planned Parenthood]] chapter "dies of a botched abortion". The anonymous aides also claimed that Peltola had used a homophobic slur, shoved a congressional aide, and ordered congressional aides to feed crickets to her family's pet chameleon. The Peltola campaign claimed that some of the allegations were false or exaggerated, and said that many of the alleged outbursts could be attributed to stress from the death of Peltola's husband and mother while she was serving in Congress.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Browning |first1=Kellen |title=Angry Outbursts, Slurs, and Firings: Tumult Rocks a Key Senate Campaign |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/23/us/politics/mary-peltola-alaska-senate-race.html |access-date=September 27, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=September 23, 2026}}</ref> The [[Alaska Democratic Party]] and Republican senator [[Lisa Murkowski]] also defended Peltola, while Senator Sullivan and the [[Alaska Republican Party]] condemned her. Alaskan political analysts and operatives interviewed by the ''[[Anchorage Daily News]]'' did not believe the allegations would have a major impact on the election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kanagy |first1=Mary |title=How ex-staffers' allegations of hostile behavior by Peltola might affect Alaska's key U.S. Senate race |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/26/how-ex-staffers-allegations-of-hostile-behavior-by-peltola-might-affect-alaskas-key-us-senate-race/ |access-date=September 27, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=September 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260926191309/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/09/26/how-ex-staffers-allegations-of-hostile-behavior-by-peltola-might-affect-alaskas-key-us-senate-race/ |archive-date=September 26, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In late October, the [[U.S. Department of Justice]] filed a {{va|lawsuit}} seeking to block the election results if Dan S. Sullivan loses by a small margin, arguing this outcome could result from him sharing a name with another candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/23/us/politics/justice-dept-alaska-senate-race-sullivan.html |title=On Trump's Order, Justice Dept. Seeks to Block Alaska Vote if Republican Loses |last1=Barrett |first1=Devlin |last2=Browning |first2=Kellen |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |date=October 23, 2026 |access-date=October 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/justice-department-sues-to-block-alaska-senate-results-if-gop-candidate-loses-c2597f6c |title=Trump Says He Directed Justice Department to Sue Alaska Over Senate Race |last1=Timms |first1=Mariah |last2=Radnofsky |first2=Louise |last3=Wise |first3=Lindsay |newspaper=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |date=October 23, 2026 |access-date=October 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Wolfe |first=Jan |date=October 23, 2026 |title=US Justice Dept sues to block possible Alaska election of Democrat to Senate |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/trump-administration-sues-block-senate-race-certification-alaska-if-republican-2026-10-23/ |access-date=October 24, 2026 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Blake |first=Aaron |date=October 23, 2026 |title=Analysis: Why Trump's dubious election lawsuits in Alaska and California should raise eyebrows |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/23/politics/trump-election-lawsuits-alaska-california |access-date=October 24, 2026 |work=CNN |language=en}}</ref> A professor of law has suggested that the suit seems to lack merit and stated that the "statute simply doesn't let the Department of Justice enjoin certification" and that the "lawsuit could well be mooted by a wide Democratic victory or a narrow Republican one, and if not, the court will likely dismiss it next month".<ref>{{cite web|last=Riccardi|first=Nicholas|url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-senate-race-sullivan-name-lawsuit-certification-d8f4a3c7b5247cc427a05f796d7c0d82|title=Trump administration sues to block possible Democratic win in Alaska Senate race|work=Associated Press|date=October 23, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Barrett |first=Devlin |date=October 23, 2026 |title=The Justice Department argues that confusion over the Alaska race's two Dan Sullivans could taint a narrow result. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/10/23/us/midterm-elections-trump |access-date=October 23, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> [[Vanita Gupta]], a former [[U.S. Associate Attorney General]], had similar views on the lack of merit, basing it on the power granted to states to determine their own elections.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Sneed|first1=Tierney|last2=Cohen|first2=Ethan|date=October 23, 2026 |title=Trump DOJ sues to block Alaska US Senate results if Republican incumbent loses |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/10/23/politics/doj-sues-alaska-dan-sullivan-us-senate |access-date=November 7, 2026 |work=CNN |language=en}}</ref> It was dismissed by U.S. District Court Judge [[Sharon L. Gleason|Sharon Gleason]] on November 1.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Riccardi |first1=Nicholas |last2=Bohrer |first2=Becky |date=November 1, 2026 |title=Judge dismisses lawsuit seeking to block certification of Alaska Senate race if GOP incumbent loses |website=[[AP News]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-senate-dan-sullivan-peltola-investigation-b35acffc8b55ef58679d9b8395760f98 }}</ref> On October 26, the ''[[Alaska Beacon]]'' reported that Dan J. Sullivan had switched his registered party affiliation from Republican to "[[Independent politician|Undeclared]]" when requesting an absentee ballot for the August primary.<ref name="Undeclared">{{cite news |last1=Brooks |first1=James |title=Dan J. Sullivan switches from Republican to 'undeclared' ahead of general election |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/10/26/dan-j-sullivan-switches-from-republican-to-undeclared-ahead-of-general-election/ |access-date=October 31, 2026 |work=[[Alaska Beacon]] |date=October 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261101035726/https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/10/26/dan-j-sullivan-switches-from-republican-to-undeclared-ahead-of-general-election/ |archive-date=November 1, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On October 28, it was reported by ''The New York Times'' that, according to Peltola's former campaign manager and longtime aide Elisa Rios, Peltola's campaign, the [[Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee]], and Democratic political operative Jerid Kurtz spent months on a "ballot composition project" to recruit [[Spoiler effect|spoiler]] candidates. This included finding a person with the same name as Senator Dan S. Sullivan, with that person ultimately being Dan J. Sullivan. Kurtz also allegedly traveled Alaska attempting to recruit other candidates. The project also included ultimately abandoned efforts to create a "[[MAGA]] party" that would further dilute Republican support. Rios also claimed Peltola was not involved but she was "definitely aware" of the project. A week before the article was published, "Ms. Rios ... contacted ''The Times''", trying to retract "all statements made regarding the 2026 Alaska Senate Race".<!-- who is being quoted here? --> ''The Times'' ran the article anyway, disclosing that "''The Times''{{'}}s standards do not obligate reporters to comply with such requests." ''The Times'' said that her "interviews were consistent with ''The Times''{{'}}s other reporting."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/28/us/politics/alaska-dan-sullivan-conspiracy-peltola.html |title=Inside Democrats' Secret Effort to Stack Alaska's Ballot |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |last=Browning |first=Kellen |date=October 28, 2026 |access-date=October 28, 2026 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Later that night Alaska Governor [[Mike Dunleavy (politician)|Mike Dunleavy]] announced a state criminal investigation into Peltola's campaign.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Browning |first1=Kellen |last2=Griffin |first2=Anna |date=October 29, 2026 |title=Alaska Governor Orders Criminal Investigation Into Peltola Campaign |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/28/us/politics/trump-peltola-sullivan-alaska-senate.html |access-date=October 29, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> On October 30, Alaska U.S. Attorney Michael Heyman told reporters in Anchorage that a federal investigation had already been underway "for several months".<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Bohrer |first1=Becky |last2=Goodman |first2=Joshua |last3=Richer |first3=Alanna |date=October 30, 2026 |title=Top federal prosecutor in Alaska says criminal probe underway over would-be spoiler Senate candidate |work=AP News |url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-sullivan-peltola-senate-election-88259e40d70a7e4f535e01a2b88489aa|access-date=November 7, 2026}}</ref> Peltola denied any knowledge of an effort to recruit another Dan Sullivan.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Chang |first1=Emily|last2=Mallin|first2=Alexander |title=Peltola denies knowledge of 'any other candidate' on Alaska Senate ballot besides herself and incumbent Dan Sullivan |url=https://abcnews.com/Politics/peltola-denies-knowledge-candidate-alaska-senate-ballot-incumbent/story?id=137705982 |access-date=November 7, 2026 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> Several donors who had given money to Dan J. Sullivan's campaign reported being visited by [[FBI]] agents.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Goodman |first1=Joshua |last2=Mustian |first2=Jim |title=FBI visits Dan J. Sullivan donor in Petersburg as Alaska Senate race inquiry widens |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/10/31/fbi-visits-dan-j-sullivan-donor-in-petersburg-as-alaska-senate-race-inquiry-widens/ |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |date=October 31, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261101040311/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/10/31/fbi-visits-dan-j-sullivan-donor-in-petersburg-as-alaska-senate-race-inquiry-widens/ |archive-date=November 1, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Heikes, the fourth candidate in the race, also reported being visited by FBI agents who served him with a [[subpoena]] to appear in federal court.<ref name="Trump" />

The controversy over Dan J. Sullivan's candidacy entered the debate over [[2026 Alaska Measure 2]], to be voted on the same day as the Senate election, which proposed to change the system for future Alaska elections. Supporters of the measure said that abolishing the open primary would make it harder to add same-name spoiler candidates to the ballot, while opponents of the measure said the ranked-choice vote reduces the spoiler effect (in this case, by allowing voters to rank both Sullivans ahead of Peltola).<ref>{{cite news |newspaper=The New York Times |title=Did the Peltola Campaign Seal the Fate of Alaska's Ranked Choice Vote? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/01/us/alaska-ranked-choice-voting-peltola.html |date=November 1, 2026 |first=Anna |last=Griffin }}</ref> Measure 2 ultimately failed; Alaskans chose to retain ranked-choice voting by a six-point margin.{{cn|date=November 2026}}

