=== Results ===
Mail-in absentee ballots, if postmarked by election day, may arrive at the election office at most 15 days afterward and still be counted.<ref>{{cite web|access-date=November 9, 2026 |title=Absentee Ballot Deadlines - Vote.org |url=https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot-deadlines |website=vote.org}}<!-- auto-translated from German by Module:CS1 translator --></ref> Within the first week, the Alaskan government had tabulated first-choice results from about 74% of the ballots. Additionally, they released the full rankings of about 200,000 ballots. To scrutinize claims that Daniel{{nbsp}}J. Sullivan{{nbsp}}Jr. functioned as a [[spoiler candidate]], ''The New York Times'' examined the ballots that had ranked him first, and found that the most frequent second-choice rank among those ballots was Peltola, not Dan{{nbsp}}S. Sullivan. In addition, only half (50.3%) of Heikes' ballots went on to name either of the Sullivans second, and some Heikes supporters named Peltola second. The ''Times'' noted that president [[Donald Trump]] desired to [[Election interference|interfere in the election]] by having the ballots that ranked either Heikes or Daniel{{nbsp}}J. Sullivan{{nbsp}}Jr. first "be immediately given to the REAL Dan{{nbsp}}[S.] Sullivan",<ref name="BrowningNYT">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/05/us/politics/alaska-senate-peltola-sullivan-what-to-know.html|title=Trump Takes Fresh Aim at Alaska, Still Counting Its Senate Results|last=Browning|work=The New York Times |quote=Mr. Trump’s call to interfere with the results of Alaska’s election stood in contrast to his relatively muted response to Democratic dominance in other elections this week.}}</ref> but even so, such an attempt would likely still result in Peltola prevailing numerically.<ref name="ZhangNYT">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/05/us/alaska-ranked-choice-data-sullivan-peltola.html|last1=Zhang|first1=Christine|last2=Vrotsos|first2=Luke|title=In Alaska, Ranked-Choice Ballots Are Unlikely to Be Enough for the Republican|work=The New York Times |date=November 5, 2026|access-date=November 7, 2026}}</ref> On November 14, ''[[NBC News]]'' projected that Peltola was likely to win the race, a characterization which the organization announces if they have at least 95% certainty that a candidate will win their race. Their projection was based on Daniel S. Sullivan's underwhelming first-choice ranking performance, combined with his "effectively closed" path to win through the ranked choice tabulation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lapinski |first1=John |last2=Pettigrew |first2=Stephen |last3=Trussler |first3=Marc |title=Why Mary Peltola is on track to win in Alaska, even if ranked choice ballots come into play |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/mary-peltola-track-win-alaska-even-ranked-choice-ballots-come-play-rcna609299 |access-date=November 16, 2026 |work=NBC News |date=November 14, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> On November 17, after three further days of counting during which Peltola exceeded 50% of the total votes cast, NBC<ref name="nbcnov17">{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/mary-peltola-democrat-wins-alaska-senate-dan-sullivan-gop-rcna603307|title=Democrat Mary Peltola flips Alaska Senate seat, defeating GOP Sen. Dan Sullivan|publisher=[[NBC News]]|last=Allen|first=Jonathan|date=November 17, 2026|access-date=November 18, 2026}}</ref> was joined by the [[Associated Press]]<ref>{{Cite news|date=November 18, 2026|title=Why AP called the Alaska Senate race for Democrat Mary Peltola |url=https://apnews.com/article/alaska-senate-peltola-sullivan-a2aa5e50cd22783b6f539a576c75aeb3 |access-date=November 18, 2026 |work=Associated Press |language=en}}</ref> and [[CNN]] in calling the race for Peltola.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Svitek |first=Patrick |date=November 18, 2026 |title=Democrat Mary Peltola will oust GOP Sen. Dan Sullivan in Alaska Senate race, CNN projects |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/17/politics/mary-peltola-wins-alaska-senate |access-date=November 18, 2026 |work=CNN |language=en}}</ref>

